<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Autonomist Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Partido Autonomista Nacional</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autonomist-party-dalmatia">Autonomist Party</a> was the conservative party that dominated Argentine politics from 1874 until 1916, led above all by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-argentino-roca">Julio Argentino Roca</a>. It ruled through an alliance of provincial governors and limited, often fraudulent elections, and it presided over the country's export boom and mass immigration until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saenz-pena-law">Sáenz Peña Law</a> opened the way to Radical victory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-autonomist-party.jpg" alt="National Autonomist Party" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HapHaxion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblema_del_Partido_Autonomista_Nacional.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was created on 15 March 1874 by the union of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autonomist-party-dalmatia">Autonomist Party</a> of Adolfo Alsina and the National Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda">Nicolás Avellaneda</a>, who was its first president of the republic, from 1874 to 1880. The political scientist Luis L. Schenoni places its emergence within a wider convergence of autonomistas and nacionalistas after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>, and argues that the party helped to consolidate the state-building coalition that came out of it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-argentino-roca">Julio Argentino Roca</a> took the presidency in 1880 under the motto &quot;peace and administration&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In economic matters the party promoted a model of agricultural exports that favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and cereal producers of the Pampas and was central to the building of the Argentine railways. It has been described as an &quot;organ of the elite, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> of wealth and power.&quot; All the presidents from 1874 to 1916 came from its ranks: after Avellaneda and Roca came <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-juarez-celman">Miguel Juárez Celman</a>, Carlos Pellegrini, Luis Sáenz Peña, José Evaristo Uriburu, Roca again from 1898 to 1904, Manuel Quintana, José Figueroa Alcorta, Roque Sáenz Peña and Victorino de la Plaza.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolution-of-the-park">Revolución del Parque</a> of 1890 a current formed within the party against the policies of Roca, known as its modernist line. Its leading figures, among them Roque Sáenz Peña, Carlos Pellegrini and Ramón J. Cárcano, sought to modernise the country's institutions and to open a true democratic system without the electoral fraud that kept the party in power. Its main opponent was the Radical Civic Union, founded after the 1890 revolution. Under Roque Sáenz Peña a law of secret universal suffrage was written, which made the free elections of 1916 possible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the electoral reform of 1912 and the Radical victory of 1916 the party broke apart. Much of it reorganised as the Conservative Party, and another faction gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-progressive-party">Democratic Progressive Party</a>, which still exists. In 1931, after the military coup of the previous year, the conservatives returned to power as the National Democratic Party, which led the Concordancia coalition. These traditional conservative forces were pushed to the margins after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a>, and the National Democratic Party fell apart after 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Autonomist Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1880 Roca’s <b>National Autonomist Party</b> dominated a conservative order sustained by limited and often fraudulent elections.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
